          %h1#continuous_maps Continuous maps
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_traditional_topology"} In traditional topology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_cohesive_homotopy_type_theory"} In cohesive homotopy type theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#for_the_set_of_real_numbers"} For the set of real numbers
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#EpsilonticDefinition"} The epsilontic definition for metric spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#for_topological_spaces"} For topological spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#further_variants"} Further variants
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#properties_preserved"} Properties preserved
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#special_maps"} Special maps
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#special_cases_in_specific_contexts"} Special cases in specific contexts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#in_constructive_mathematics"} In constructive mathematics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#literature"} Literature
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p There are multiple different concepts of continuous maps, even in classical mathematics, depending upon which foundation one is using.
          %h3#in_traditional_topology In traditional topology
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
            is called
            %em continuous
            if its values
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            do not “jump” with variation of its argument
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            itself “jumps”. Roughly speaking, if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≈
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \approx x_2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_1) \approx f(x_2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            if care is taken about the domains of these variables.)
          %p
            In order to make this precise (in standard analysis) one needs some concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhoods"} neighbourhoods
            of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %p
            For instance if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            carry structure of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is continuous if for every point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            and for every small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
            around its image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            which is still mapped by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            into that target open ball. This definition turns out to have more elegant formulation that needs to mention neither the points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            nor the radii of open balls around points: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            induces a concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is continuous precisely if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimages"} preimages
            under
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are still open subsets in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This then is the general definition of continuity of a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            between
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %p
            A function between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is
            %em continuous
            precisely if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimages"} preimages
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            are again open subsets.
          %p
            Continuous maps are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            forms a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are the continuous functions.
          %p
            Further generalization of the concept of continuity exists, for instance to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            (and then to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+spaces"} convergence spaces
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+space"} continuous space
          %h3#in_cohesive_homotopy_type_theory In cohesive homotopy type theory
          %p
            There is also a concept of continuous function in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#for_the_set_of_real_numbers For the set of real numbers
          %p
            Given a bounded or unbounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+interval"} open
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:I \to \mathbb{R}
            A function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:I \to \mathbb{R}
            is continuous if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+of+a+function"} graph
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            defined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi f
                    %mo :
